WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.420
App registrations define how an application integrates with the Microsoft identity platform.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:16.200
They include an application client ID, supported account types, redirect URIs, credentials, exposed APIs, requested permissions, and ownership metadata.

3
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.340
A registration is not just an administrative record.

4
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.600
It is part of the application security configuration.

5
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.440
Enterprise applications and service principals represent application instances in tenants.

6
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:40.140
In practical terms, the app registration describes the application, while the service principal is the tenant-local object through which that application is used, consented to, assigned, and managed.

7
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:46.240
This distinction matters for multitenant applications, customer onboarding, access review, and cleanup.

8
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.000
Redirect URIs should be narrow, environment-specific, and reviewed before release.

9
00:00:51.100 --> 00:01:02.140
Development, staging, and production registrations should not be casually mixed, because a convenience setting in a test environment can become a production risk when copied forward.

10
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:10.720
Local development redirect URIs, preview domains, old test hosts, and unused callbacks should be reviewed and removed when they are no longer needed.

11
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:19.400
Single-tenant and multitenant decisions affect issuer validation, consent, onboarding, support, and operations.

12
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.540
A single-tenant app can make stronger assumptions about the tenant it accepts.

13
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:35.600
A multitenant app needs deliberate issuer handling and a governance process for which tenants are allowed, how consent is granted, and how customer-specific configuration is reviewed.

14
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.680
App registration ownership should be clear.

15
00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:50.700
Teams should know who approves permissions, rotates credentials, reviews redirect URIs, removes stale credentials, responds to alerts, and retires abandoned applications.

16
00:01:50.800 --> 00:02:00.240
Unused credentials, excessive permissions, missing owners, and stale settings are maintenance issues that can turn into security issues.
